# FabricXAI — production environment. Copy to `.env.production` and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.production.example .env.production
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env.production up -d
#
# `.env.production` is gitignored. Every value marked GENERATE must be generated on the
# host and never reused between deployments — a shared secret between a staging box and a
# factory's production is one box away from being a breach of the factory's payroll.
#
# Compose fails to start if any `:?` variable is missing, which is deliberate: a
# deployment that comes up with a default password is worse than one that refuses to
# come up.

# ── Identity ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The bare hostname. Caddy requests its certificate for exactly this, so it must
# already resolve to this host before the first `up`.
APP_DOMAIN=erp.example.com
# The same host as an https:// URL. This is what every emailed link is built from
# (settings/policies.ts) and what Better Auth derives secure-cookie behaviour from — an
# http:// value here silently produces non-secure session cookies.
APP_URL=https://erp.example.com
# Let's Encrypt writes here about expiry and revocation. Use a mailbox somebody reads.
TLS_EMAIL=it@example.com

# ── The image to run ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pin by DIGEST, not by tag. `:latest` means "whatever was pushed last", which during an
# incident is the question you least want to be asking.
IMAGE=ghcr.io/your-org/fabricxai@sha256:REPLACE_ME

# ── Secrets · GENERATE all of these ────────────────────────────────────────────
# openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
# openssl rand -base64 24 — the owner/migration role. Used ONLY by the migrate service.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# openssl rand -base64 24 — the application role. This is the connection RLS binds; the
# app refuses to boot if it turns out to be the owner or to hold BYPASSRLS.
APP_DB_PASSWORD=
# openssl rand -base64 24 — the pooler's lookup account. The only credential written to
# the pooler's disk, and it can do nothing but call app.pgbouncer_get_auth.
PGBOUNCER_AUTH_PASSWORD=
# openssl rand -base64 24
REDIS_PASSWORD=
# openssl rand -base64 24 — MinIO root, which is also the app's S3 credential.
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=

# ── Names (defaults are fine) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
POSTGRES_USER=fabricxai
POSTGRES_DB=fabricxai
APP_DB_USER=fabricxai_app_rw
MINIO_ROOT_USER=fabricxai
S3_BUCKET=fabricxai
S3_REGION=us-east-1

# Written by docs/runbooks/deploy.md from PGBOUNCER_AUTH_PASSWORD. Keep it outside the
# repo tree if you can; it is mounted read-only either way.
PGBOUNCER_USERLIST=./secrets/pgbouncer-userlist.txt

# ── Email ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Mailpit is dev-only. Verification email is REQUIRED to sign in, so a deployment with
# no working mail path is a deployment nobody can log into.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
EMAIL_FROM=FabricXAI <no-reply@example.com>
# Optional: if set, used instead of SMTP.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# ── Observability ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL. Set it and errors are aggregated and alertable; leave it and the app says so
# at startup and logs to stdout only. Logs are structured JSON (pino) either way — ship
# them somewhere, because unshipped logs on a VPS are logs nobody reads.
SENTRY_DSN=

# ── Health ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Three endpoints, three questions (plan 7.5):
#
#   /api/health       is this process alive        — Docker's HEALTHCHECK. No detail.
#   /api/ready        can it serve a request       — Caddy's upstream check. Postgres and
#                                                    Redis through the pooler; ok and a
#                                                    latency, never an exception string.
#   /api/health/jobs  is the schedule still firing — YOU, with this token.
#
# The worker serves no HTTP and its container healthcheck is disabled, so this route is the
# only way to see that its schedule has stopped. It names every scheduled task and what is
# broken, which is reconnaissance rather than a status page — so with this unset the route
# REFUSES with a 503 rather than falling open.
#
#   HEALTH_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 24)
#   curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer $HEALTH_TOKEN" https://<domain>/api/health/jobs | jq
HEALTH_TOKEN=

# ── Tuning ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Jobs processed concurrently per worker. 4 suits a 4-vCPU VPS; raising it past the
# PgBouncer pool (25) just moves the queue.
WORKER_CONCURRENCY=4

# ── MARBIM ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave MARBIM_ENABLED=false until a real provider is registered (audit AI-B1). Enabled
# with no provider means the copilot looks available and fails per use, which is the worst
# of the three states — a factory should be told it is off.
#
# With MARBIM_ENABLED=true, production requires ANTHROPIC_API_KEY specifically — not just
# any one of the three (plan 6.4). The assistant button on every screen and /marbim do
# exactly one thing: ask the REASON model. A Gemini-only deployment therefore ships a
# copilot that renders perfectly and fails on every question.
#
# The other two are optional and each buys one capability, refusing by name when absent:
#   GEMINI  → reading documents. The only vendor of the three returning per-token
#             log-probabilities, so the only one an extraction can derive a MEASURED
#             per-field confidence from (plan 6.3 removed the invented alternative).
#   OPENAI  → style fingerprints for Order Memory's similarity search.
MARBIM_ENABLED=false
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
GEMINI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
